s paper needs substantial revisions that cannot be completed by the next ARR cycle.\n\n1 = Do not resubmit: This paper has to be fully redone, or it is not relevant to the *ACL community (e.g. it is in no way related to computational processing of language).\n\nThese definitions reflect the ARR's venue-based ranking system, where scores correspond to different publication venues from top-tier conferences down to workshops and rejection categories."],"durationSeconds":13.953177644999997}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0136oWJKKUu4EZpwgqz9mFyQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the ARR review form scoring rubric:\n\n**Overall Assessment (1-5 with half points):**\n- 5 = Consider for Award\n- 4.5 = Borderline Award\n- 4 = Conference\n- 3.5 = Borderline Conference\n- 3 = Findings\n- 2.5 = Borderline Findings\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1.5 = Resubmit after next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n\n**Soundness (1-5):**\n\n\n- 5 = Excellent\n- 4 = Strong\n- 3 = Acceptable\n- 2 = Poor\n- 1 = Major Issues\n\n**Excitement (1-5):**\n- 5 = Highly Exciting\n- 4 = Exciting\n- 3 = Interesting but not exciting\n- 2 = Marginally interesting\n- 1 = Not interesting\n\n**Confidence (1-5):**\n- 5 = Positive\n- 4 = Quite sure\n- 3 = Pretty sure\n- 2 = Willing to defend\n- 1 = Not my area\n\nI'm looking at the complete evaluation criteria for EMNLP 2026 Findings, which breaks down soundness, excitement, and confidence on separate 1-5 scales alongside the overall assessment score.\n\nThis paper lands at a 3.0 for Findings, so I need to set up a structured review process with two specialized reviewers working in parallel. R1 will dig into the methodological rigor and experimental design—examining the tables, statistical tests, and whether the experiments adequately test the claims—while R2 focuses on whether the contribution is genuinely novel and how well it positions itself against existing work. I'll run them through a full roundtable discussion where they first review independently, then share their perspectives, converge on key disagreements, and synthesize a final assessment.\n\nNow I'm drafting the detailed prompts for each reviewer with the paper content, scoring rubric, and specific evaluation criteria. For R2, I need to make sure they assess the novelty of the failure mode taxonomy and diagnostic framework, and critically evaluate how this work compares to concurrent approaches like Mondorf et al., PIE, and CIRCUS.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2286,"cache_read_input_tokens":56097,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2286},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f2e9568a-8920-4443-b16c-735005e3494c","uuid":"06e9aafa-33cb-496e-907c-9046cb6b520d"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0136oWJKKUu4EZpwgqz9mFyQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have the ARR scoring rubric. Now dispatching two parallel reviewers: R1 (Methods + Experiments) and R2 (Novelty + Related Work)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2286,"cache_read_input_tokens":56097,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2286},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f2e9568a-8920-4443-b16c-735005e3494c","uuid":"0ad6c5fd-4e80-4bae-ac74-c038da2fef5d"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0136oWJKKUu4EZpwgqz9mFyQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YNTU63w58WbBxkbNpR3YCn","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a peer review of a mechanistic interpretability paper submitted to EMNLP 2026 Findings. Your focus area is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Scoring Rubric (ARR / EMNLP 2026)\n\n**Overall Assessment (1-5, half-point increments):**\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference (main track worthy)\n- 3.5 = Borderline Conference\n- 3 = Findings\n- 2.5 = Borderline Findings\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n\n**Soundness (1-5):**\n- 5 = Excellent: one of the most thorough studies I've seen\n- 4 = Strong: sufficient support for all claims, extra experiments nice but not essential\n- 3 = Acceptable: sufficient support for main claims\n- 2 = Poor: some main claims not sufficiently supported\n- 1 = Major issues\n\n**Excitement (1-5):**\n- 5 = Highly exciting, would recommend to others\n- 4 = Exciting, would mention to others\n- 3 = Interesting but not exciting\n- 2 = Marginally interesting\n- 1 = Not interesting\n\n**Confidence (1-5):**\n- 5 = Positive my evaluation is correct\n- 4 = Quite sure\n- 3 = Pretty sure\n- 2 = Willing to defend\n- 1 = Not my area\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"When Does Cross-Method Consensus Improve Circuit Discovery? A Systematic Analysis of Failure Modes\"\n\n**Core Question:** Does binary consensus scoring (CSS) — intersecting the top-N edges from multiple circuit discovery methods — yield more faithful circuits?\n\n**Methods studied:** EAP-IG, Edge Pruning (EP), ACDC — K=3 methods.\n\n**Tasks/Models:** 4 tasks (IOI, Arithmetic, SVA, Factual Recall) × GPT-2 Small (32K edges), plus IOI on Qwen-0.5B (179K edges) = 5 configurations.\n\n**Key metric:** Circuit Performance Recovery (CPR) under zero ablation; bootstrap CIs (B=10,000).\n\n**Main Results:**\n1. CSS produces marginal advantage on only 1/5 configs (IOI/GPT-2: ΔCPR=0.081, p=0.089). After size control (7 edges each), Δ shrinks to 0.017, p=0.27.\n2. Three failure modes identified:\n   - FM1: ACDC binarization pathology (isinf threshold → consensus degenerates to ACDC subset)\n   - FM2: Consensus redundancy (high Jaccard >0.5 → intersection removes useful edges)\n   - FM3: Scoring granularity mismatch on Qwen-0.5B (IFR=block-level, ACDC=node-level → near-zero overlap with edge-level gradient methods)\n3. Jaccard diagnostic: max pairwise Jaccard < 0.35 predicts CSS utility (4/5 configs correct), Spearman ρ=−0.70 but p=0.19 (n=5).\n\n**Tables in the paper:**\n- Table 1: IOI size-controlled comparison (N=7), CSS=1.0 CPR=0.109, ACDC top-7=0.092, EAP-IG top-7=0.078, EP top-7=0.065, Random-7=-0.031\n- Table 2: Size confound decomposition — CSS advantage retained: 42% vs ACDC, 14% vs EAP-IG, 13% vs EP\n- Table 3: Bootstrap 95% CIs — IOI orig p=0.089 (No), size-ctrl p=0.27 (No), CSS vs EAP-IG p=0.002 (Yes* but confounded)\n- Table 4: Factual Recall — CSS=1.0 CPR=0.83 < EAP-IG=0.88\n- Table 5: Scoring granularity on Qwen-0.5B\n- Table 6: Pairwise Jaccard on Qwen-0.5B (all cross-granularity pairs ≈0)\n- Table 7: Max Jaccard vs CSS advantage across 5 configs\n\n**Limitations acknowledged:** size confound, statistical power (n=5 configs, 100 prompts), task coverage, zero ablation only, method selection (2/3 gradient → correlated).\n\n## Experiment Coverage Context\n\nThe paper's imagined/planned experiment set included 6 experiments:\n1. GPT-2 Factual Recall K=3 CSS — completed, result inconclusive, included as Table 4\n2. IOI size-controlled baseline — completed, included as Table 1-2\n3. Bootstrap CIs on CPR — completed, included as Table 3\n4. Ablation method comparison (zero vs mean) — NOT completed, mentioned qualitatively in limitations\n5. IFR vs ACDC divergence analysis on Qwen — completed (analysis only), included as Tables 5-6\n6. Jaccard-CSS scatter plot + correlation — completed, included as Table 7\n\nSo 5/6 planned experiments are covered; the ablation method comparison is missing.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focused on:\n\n1. **Methodology Assessment:**\n   - Is the CSS formulation (binary intersection) well-motivated and clearly defined?\n   - Is the binarization approach (top-N for gradient, native for ACDC) appropriate? Is the fix (uniform top-N) sufficient?\n   - Are the evaluation metrics (CPR, CMD) appropriate for the claims?\n\n2. **Experimental Rigor:**\n   - Size confound analysis: Is it thorough? Does Table 1-2 adequately address the confound?\n   - Bootstrap CIs: Are they appropriate? Is B=10,000 sufficient? Is BCa appropriate here?\n   - Statistical claims: Are they calibrated to the evidence (marginal, directional, etc.)?\n   - Is 100 prompts per task sufficient? What about prompt diversity?\n\n3. **Experimental Completeness:**\n   - The ablation method comparison (zero vs mean) is missing. How critical is this gap?\n   - Only 5 configurations. Is this sufficient for the claims made?\n   - Only one positive case (IOI) — how much can we conclude from n=1?\n   - Jaccard diagnostic at n=5 — is this even worth reporting?\n\n4. **Failure Mode Analysis:**\n   - Are the three failure modes well-diagnosed and mechanistically explained?\n   - Is the granularity mismatch analysis (FM3) convincing without new experiments?\n   - Does the CIRCUS comparison adequately control for alternative explanations?\n\n5. **Reproducibility:**\n   - Are hyperparameters (EAP-IG steps, EP training, ACDC threshold) well-specified?\n   - Is the experimental setup reproducible from the paper alone?\n\n**Output your review in this exact format:**\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences summarizing the paper's contribution from your perspective]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, 3-5 items]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, 3-5 items, each with specific evidence/reasoning]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list, 2-3 critical questions]\n\n### Missing Experiments / Suggestions\n[What additional experiments would strengthen the paper?]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Overall: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n\n### Justification\n[2-3 sentences explaining your overall score]\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2286,"cache_read_input_tokens":56097,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2286},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f2e9568a-8920-4443-b16c-735005e3494c","uuid":"be91a7a0-5aac-46e5-a199-c3da9feadb18"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a6379bd0a89e2d65e","tool_use_id":"toolu_01YNTU63w58WbBxkbNpR3YCn","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a peer review of a mechanistic interpretability paper submitted to EMNLP 2026 Findings. Your focus area is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Scoring Rubric (ARR / EMNLP 2026)\n\n**Overall Assessment (1-5, half-point increments):**\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference (main track worthy)\n- 3.5 = Borderline Conference\n- 3 = Findings\n- 2.5 = Borderline Findings\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n\n**Soundness (1-5):**\n- 5 = Excellent: one of the most thorough studies I've seen\n- 4 = Strong: sufficient support for all claims, extra experiments nice but not essential\n- 3 = Acceptable: sufficient support for main claims\n- 2 = Poor: some main claims not sufficiently supported\n- 1 = Major issues\n\n**Excitement (1-5):**\n- 5 = Highly exciting, would recommend to others\n- 4 = Exciting, would mention to others\n- 3 = Interesting but not exciting\n- 2 = Marginally interesting\n- 1 = Not interesting\n\n**Confidence (1-5):**\n- 5 = Positive my evaluation is correct\n- 4 = Quite sure\n- 3 = Pretty sure\n- 2 = Willing to defend\n- 1 = Not my area\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"When Does Cross-Method Consensus Improve Circuit Discovery? A Systematic Analysis of Failure Modes\"\n\n**Core Question:** Does binary consensus scoring (CSS) — intersecting the top-N edges from multiple circuit discovery methods — yield more faithful circuits?\n\n**Methods studied:** EAP-IG, Edge Pruning (EP), ACDC — K=3 methods.\n\n**Tasks/Models:** 4 tasks (IOI, Arithmetic, SVA, Factual Recall) × GPT-2 Small (32K edges), plus IOI on Qwen-0.5B (179K edges) = 5 configurations.\n\n**Key metric:** Circuit Performance Recovery (CPR) under zero ablation; bootstrap CIs (B=10,000).\n\n**Main Results:**\n1. CSS produces marginal advantage on only 1/5 configs (IOI/GPT-2: ΔCPR=0.081, p=0.089). After size control (7 edges each), Δ shrinks to 0.017, p=0.27.\n2. Three failure modes identified:\n   - FM1: ACDC binarization pathology (isinf threshold → consensus degenerates to ACDC subset)\n   - FM2: Consensus redundancy (high Jaccard >0.5 → intersection removes useful edges)\n   - FM3: Scoring granularity mismatch on Qwen-0.5B (IFR=block-level, ACDC=node-level → near-zero overlap with edge-level gradient methods)\n3. Jaccard diagnostic: max pairwise Jaccard < 0.35 predicts CSS utility (4/5 configs correct), Spearman ρ=−0.70 but p=0.19 (n=5).\n\n**Tables in the paper:**\n- Table 1: IOI size-controlled comparison (N=7), CSS=1.0 CPR=0.109, ACDC top-7=0.092, EAP-IG top-7=0.078, EP top-7=0.065, Random-7=-0.031\n- Table 2: Size confound decomposition — CSS advantage retained: 42% vs ACDC, 14% vs EAP-IG, 13% vs EP\n- Table 3: Bootstrap 95% CIs — IOI orig p=0.089 (No), size-ctrl p=0.27 (No), CSS vs EAP-IG p=0.002 (Yes* but confounded)\n- Table 4: Factual Recall — CSS=1.0 CPR=0.83 < EAP-IG=0.88\n- Table 5: Scoring granularity on Qwen-0.5B\n- Table 6: Pairwise Jaccard on Qwen-0.5B (all cross-granularity pairs ≈0)\n- Table 7: Max Jaccard vs CSS advantage across 5 configs\n\n**Limitations acknowledged:** size confound, statistical power (n=5 configs, 100 prompts), task coverage, zero ablation only, method selection (2/3 gradient → correlated).\n\n## Experiment Coverage Context\n\nThe paper's imagined/planned experiment set included 6 experiments:\n1. GPT-2 Factual Recall K=3 CSS — completed, result inconclusive, included as Table 4\n2. IOI size-controlled baseline — completed, included as Table 1-2\n3. Bootstrap CIs on CPR — completed, included as Table 3\n4. Ablation method comparison (zero vs mean) — NOT completed, mentioned qualitatively in limitations\n5. IFR vs ACDC divergence analysis on Qwen — completed (analysis only), included as Tables 5-6\n6. Jaccard-CSS scatter plot + correlation — completed, included as Table 7\n\nSo 5/6 planned experiments are covered; the ablation method comparison is missing.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focused on:\n\n1. **Methodology Assessment:**\n   - Is the CSS formulation (binary intersection) well-motivated and clearly defined?\n   - Is the binarization approach (top-N for gradient, native for ACDC) appropriate? Is the fix (uniform top-N) sufficient?\n   - Are the evaluation metrics (CPR, CMD) appropriate for the claims?\n\n2. **Experimental Rigor:**\n   - Size confound analysis: Is it thorough? Does Table 1-2 adequately address the confound?\n   - Bootstrap CIs: Are they appropriate? Is B=10,000 sufficient? Is BCa appropriate here?\n   - Statistical claims: Are they calibrated to the evidence (marginal, directional, etc.)?\n   - Is 100 prompts per task sufficient? What about prompt diversity?\n\n3. **Experimental Completeness:**\n   - The ablation method comparison (zero vs mean) is missing. How critical is this gap?\n   - Only 5 configurations. Is this sufficient for the claims made?\n   - Only one positive case (IOI) — how much can we conclude from n=1?\n   - Jaccard diagnostic at n=5 — is this even worth reporting?\n\n4. **Failure Mode Analysis:**\n   - Are the three failure modes well-diagnosed and mechanistically explained?\n   - Is the granularity mismatch analysis (FM3) convincing without new experiments?\n   - Does the CIRCUS comparison adequately control for alternative explanations?\n\n5. **Reproducibility:**\n   - Are hyperparameters (EAP-IG steps, EP training, ACDC threshold) well-specified?\n   - Is the experimental setup reproducible from the paper alone?\n\n**Output your review in this exact format:**\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences summarizing the paper's contribution from your perspective]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, 3-5 items]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, 3-5 items, each with specific evidence/reasoning]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list, 2-3 critical questions]\n\n### Missing Experiments / Suggestions\n[What additional experiments would strengthen the paper?]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Overall: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n\n### Justification\n[2-3 sentences explaining your overall score]\n```\n","uuid":"fd6f7ec1-b901-41be-9688-4256b9cae5b8","session_id":"f2e9568a-8920-4443-b16c-735005e3494c"}
